@startuml
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam ArrowColor black
skinparam nodesep 35
skinparam ranksep 45
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center
skinparam rectangle {
  BackgroundColor white
  BorderColor #90A4AE
  FontColor #455A64
}
skinparam card {
  BackgroundColor #E8F4FD
  BorderColor #2196F3
  FontColor #1565C0
}

title ttt — why the newline matters\nTCP does not deliver messages. It delivers bytes. The newline is what puts the messages back.

card sends [
**1. The player sends three messages** — three separate calls to ""write_all""
====
|<#BBDEFB> ""HELLO<U+005C>n"" |<#FFE0B2> ""MOVE 5<U+005C>n"" |<#C8E6C9> ""BYE<U+005C>n"" |
|<#BBDEFB> 6 bytes |<#FFE0B2> 7 bytes |<#C8E6C9> 4 bytes |
]

card stream [
**2. What TCP carries** — 17 bytes in a row, with no marks between the messages
====
|<#BBDEFB> 48 |<#BBDEFB> 45 |<#BBDEFB> 4C |<#BBDEFB> 4C |<#BBDEFB> 4F |<#BBDEFB> **0A** |<#FFE0B2> 4D |<#FFE0B2> 4F |<#FFE0B2> 56 |<#FFE0B2> 45 |<#FFE0B2> 20 |<#FFE0B2> 35 |<#FFE0B2> **0A** |<#C8E6C9> 42 |<#C8E6C9> 59 |<#C8E6C9> 45 |<#C8E6C9> **0A** |
|<#BBDEFB> H |<#BBDEFB> E |<#BBDEFB> L |<#BBDEFB> L |<#BBDEFB> O |<#BBDEFB> **<U+005C>n** |<#FFE0B2> M |<#FFE0B2> O |<#FFE0B2> V |<#FFE0B2> E |<#FFE0B2> sp |<#FFE0B2> 5 |<#FFE0B2> **<U+005C>n** |<#C8E6C9> B |<#C8E6C9> Y |<#C8E6C9> E |<#C8E6C9> **<U+005C>n** |
----
The colours are only in this drawing. On the wire there is nothing
but those 17 bytes — the **0A** newlines are the only real separators.
]

card reads [
**3. What the server's reads actually return** — chunks the network chose, not messages
====
|= read |= bytes |= what came back |
| 1st | 9 | ""HELLO<U+005C>nMOV"" |
| 2nd | 5 | ""E 5<U+005C>nB"" |
| 3rd | 3 | ""YE<U+005C>n"" |
----
A message was **split across two reads**, and one read held the
**end of one message and the start of another**. This is normal TCP
behaviour, not an error — and it changes from run to run.
]

card lines [
**4. ""BufReader::lines()"" puts them back** — it buffers, and cuts at every newline
====
|<#BBDEFB> ""HELLO"" |<#FFE0B2> ""MOVE 5"" |<#C8E6C9> ""BYE"" |
----
Exactly three lines, whatever the chunking was.
The newline itself is removed — it did its job.
Each line then goes to ""wire::decode_request"".
]

sends -down-> stream : ""write_all"" copies the\nbytes into the stream
stream -down-> reads : the network hands over\nwhatever it has, when it has it
reads -down-> lines : the ""BufReader"" keeps\nthe leftovers until a newline arrives

note right of reads
  **This is the bug you would write by hand.**
  ""socket.read(&mut buf)"" once, then decoding
  ""buf"" as if it were one message, works on
  localhost and fails on a real network.
  We never call ""read"" directly: ""lines()""
  is what makes the framing correct.
end note

@enduml
